oon or linkage are the genetic  forces  that underlie the trade-off but  epistasis and gye  and also important factors that add  to the ignorant variability of this  phenomenon  since we already know that the effects  of disease resistance are variable  and are case specific i am focusing on  three  uh specific or overarching questions  to measure the direction on magnitude  of the potential effect of energy  resistance  specifically that is is it positive  is it negative or is it neutral  i am quantifying these potential effects  by  evaluating crop performance in the  presence of the disease the artificially  inoculated condition  and in the absence of the disease the  uninoculated  counterpart i am initially focusing on  three agronomic traits  yield stock lodging and maturity  i'm also interested in investigating  what are the effects of selection for  yield on nlp resistance and trade-offs  the problem is that through natural and  artificial selection  farmers and plant breeders have been  strongly selecting for  resistance alleles whose benefits  are the way the cost is biologically  sound  however this logic hasn't been tested  experimentally i wish to test this  hypothesis  taking advantage of the genomes to field  initiative  which is a testing hybrid populations  in many environments in north america  although my nlb trial  was planted over six years in a single  location here in new york estate  the g2f initiative provides unique  opportunities  to study several aspects of nlp  resistance  uh in mace hybrids so in the next slides  i will present my results  on agronomic effects association studies  and high throughput phenotyping  in the 2014 population alone  it seems that nlp resistance has no  yield penalty uh or in the  in the absence of the dc as revealed by  this a positive  slope from a regression between the  grain yield  measured in the uninoculated trial  and energy resistance as measured in the  inoculated  trial i observe a similar tendency  a